<!--
  Navigate-to-pose, replanning only when the current path becomes invalid, with
  contextual recovery. Based on nav2's
  navigate_w_replanning_only_if_path_becomes_invalid.xml but without its outer
  system-level RecoveryNode: a planner / controller failure clears its own
  costmap and retries once, since stale obstacles in the costmap are the most
  common cause. nav2's system-level branch (clear both costmaps,
  Spin/Wait/BackUp via RoundRobin, abort on GoalUpdated) is left out for now;
  most of its BT nodes and the behavior server are not ported yet.

  Why not replan every tick (this tree did, at 1 Hz, until 2026-10): a fresh
  plan always wins, so the route is only as stable as the costmap's memory of
  what it has seen. The global costmap's obstacle layer raytraces the 3D cloud
  in 2D, so a ray to a wall point above a low blocker clears the blocker's cell
  as soon as the robot can see past it. Half-way round a detour the original
  route then read free and shortest again, and the robot turned back into the
  blocker. Now a path is kept until IsPathValid sees, on the part still ahead,
  a centre cell at >= INSCRIBED cost (the planner's own rejection test) or a
  LETHAL cell under the padded footprint's perimeter at the path heading
  +-0.35 rad (the test RPP applies before it drives, run on the global costmap
  while the blocker is still 2.5 m out -- without it a blocker 0.25-0.45 m
  beside the route passed the centre test, RPP refused the path, and the goal
  aborted with no detour), or the goal changes. The price: a blocker that
  leaves does not shorten the route back; the detour is finished. That is the
  recoverable side, and no "switch when the new path is X% shorter" threshold
  had to be invented to get it.

  The 1 Hz RateController now paces validity checks; a plan only runs when the
  check fails, so a blocked path is still noticed within ~1 s of the global
  costmap (also 1 Hz) marking it. IsPathValid gets 100 ms instead of the 20 ms
  default_server_timeout: it answers after taking the costmap lock, which a
  costmap update can hold for longer than 20 ms. A timeout keeps the path (a
  replan nobody can justify is the thing this tree exists to avoid; the next
  check is a second away and RPP holds the robot meanwhile). A timeout WARN in
  the task_runner log on every tick means the costmap update and the check
  have phase-locked: raise server_timeout here.

  FollowPath gets three retries, and they refill (retry_refill_time, 2026-10).
  The count is how long the robot waits for a blocker that stands in front of
  it: this stack has no local avoidance (RPP is a tracker) and no Wait
  recovery, so once RPP refuses to drive, the wait is failure_tolerance (3 s)
  times retries + 1, with a local costmap clear between attempts (re-marked in
  0.2 s; RPP zeroes its accel baseline on every refusal, so no lurch). One
  retry (~6 s) was enough for a person walking across and not for one
  standing and talking; three (~12 s) covers that, at the price of a task
  failure taking ~12 s instead of ~6 s to declare. The progress checker's
  30 s movement_time_allowance remains the outer bound. The refill: the
  RecoveryNode only resets when it returns, and FollowPath is RUNNING for the
  whole goal, so the retries used to be per goal -- a blocker met early used
  them up and the next one, however much later, failed the goal outright. An
  attempt that ran at least 60 s before failing now earns them back. 60 s is
  twice movement_time_allowance: an attempt that never gets going fails
  sooner than that (3 s of failure_tolerance, or ~30 s without progress), so
  a dead end still runs out of retries; it must not drop to 30 or below. The
  planner branch's RecoveryNode keeps one retry and needs no refill:
  ComputePathToPose returns each tick, which resets that node.

  Costmap clear services live on the costmap nodes owned by the planner
  (global_costmap) and controller (local_costmap). Relative service names
  resolve under the robot namespace because every node in the stack shares it.
-->
<root main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <PipelineSequence name="NavigateWithReplanning">
      <RateController hz="1.0">
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToPose">
          <Fallback name="FallbackComputePathToPose">
            <ReactiveSequence name="CheckIfNewPathNeeded">
              <Inverter>
                <GlobalUpdatedGoal/>
              </Inverter>
              <IsPathValid path="{path}" server_timeout="100"/>
            </ReactiveSequence>
            <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
          </Fallback>
          <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Context" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
        </RecoveryNode>
      </RateController>
      <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="3" retry_refill_time="60.0" name="FollowPath">
        <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="FollowPath"/>
        <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Context" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
      </RecoveryNode>
    </PipelineSequence>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
